"Among the six excesses introduced in the last lesson, Chinese medicine singles out wind for special attention, calling it \"the spearhead of the hundred diseases\" — the factor most likely to lead an attack and open the way for others to follow. The reasoning is based on a simple physical observation: wind moves things, changes direction suddenly, and rarely travels alone. In Chinese medicine's logic, an illness that behaves the same way — appearing suddenly, shifting location, or changing character quickly — is read as carrying a \"wind\" pattern, whether or not actual weather was involved. This is why wind is described as \"the spearhead\": cold, dampness, or heat rarely invade the body cleanly on their own. They are said to ride in on wind, the way dust and pollen travel on an actual breeze. A \"wind-cold\" pattern, for example, describes an illness with wind's suddenness and cold's contraction and chill combined — closer to what English speakers mean by the early, sudden stage of catching a common cold than to a philosophical metaphor. The practical value of \"wind\" as a category is that it gives Chinese medicine a name for a recognizable clinical picture: rapid onset, symptoms that move (a headache that shifts location, joint pain that travels from one joint to another), and an illness that seems to arrive from nowhere. Whether or not modern science would classify the cause the same way, the pattern being described — sudden, moving, and rarely isolated — is a genuinely useful thing to notice and name.": "83fe7c93916b",
